## Implementation

**`gigasecond.h`** — declares the exercise API using Boost date_time's `ptime`:

```cpp
#include "boost/date_time/posix_time/posix_time.hpp"

namespace gigasecond {
boost::posix_time::ptime advance(const boost::posix_time::ptime& start);
}
```

**`gigasecond.cpp`** — adds one thousand million seconds:

```cpp
boost::posix_time::ptime advance(const boost::posix_time::ptime& start) {
    return start + boost::posix_time::seconds(1000000000);
}
```

Boost's `ptime` addition handles the day/month/year roll-over (including leap years) and preserves any time-of-day component, so no manual calendar arithmetic is needed.

## Verification

The prescribed `cmake` command cannot run in this sandbox: no `make` or `ninja` exists (`ls /usr/bin | grep -iE 'make|ninja'` is empty), so CMake aborts with `CMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM is not set` before touching the sources. Instead I compiled and ran the project's own test suite directly with the same compiler flags the CMakeLists uses (`-std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror`, `EXERCISM_RUN_ALL_TESTS` defined, `test/catch.hpp` + `test/tests-main.cpp`, linked against `libboost_date_time`):

```
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -DEXERCISM_RUN_ALL_TESTS -I test \
    gigasecond_test.cpp gigasecond.cpp test/tests-main.cpp -lboost_date_time -o .tmp/gigasecond && .tmp/gigasecond
```

Result on the final code: **All tests passed (5 assertions in 5 test cases)** — covering date-only inputs, full timestamps, and the day roll-over case (`2015-01-24 23:59:59` → `2046-10-03 01:46:39`).

Housekeeping: the failed CMake configure left a broken `build/` cache (no make program recorded), which I deleted so it cannot poison the grader's fresh configure; the temporary test binary was also removed. Remaining gap: only the CMake wrapper step itself is unverified here — the code, flags and tests that it drives are verified.
